WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.500
My name is Segev Tsfuti.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.280
I'm Director of Academic Programs here at

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.635
the Stanford Center for Professional Development

00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:19.585
and I'm really excited to have you here tonight.

00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:24.550
We have gathered a very interesting group of people to share information with you,

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.690
and we'll start by just taking.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:29.970
This and then getting started.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.960
So I want to acknowledge all the SCPD team members who are working

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.720
with colleagues and other teams across the university with a single purpose in mind,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.880
and that is to give you access to

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.485
these classes that are offered every quarter here at Stanford.

00:00:44.485 --> 00:00:47.360
The result this autumn quarter,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.540
SCPD is offering 64 classes to remote students,

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.055
and we're excited to have you here and join us.

00:00:53.055 --> 00:00:54.750
As you look around the room,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.445
and as you get to know your classmates later in the welcome reception,

00:00:58.445 --> 00:01:00.770
also know that you are part of a group of more than

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.910
1,100 remote students that are joining

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.470
you this quarter from around the world and all over the country.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:11.315
[NOISE] So, on behalf of all of us at Stanford,

00:01:11.315 --> 00:01:13.010
congratulation on taking this step

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.960
in your education journey, we're really glad you're here.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.250
Our goal tonight is to share with you

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.200
the information need to know to get the quarter off to a good start.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.320
Our discussion will be mostly Q and A with a panel of experts,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.555
two returning SCPD students,

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.215
a professor, and a TA.

00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:33.460
All of whom will tell it to you simply just the way it is.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.335
[NOISE] But before we dive in,

00:01:36.335 --> 00:01:39.005
I want to introduce our keynote speaker tonight,

00:01:39.005 --> 00:01:41.045
Computer Science Professor, John Mitchell.

00:01:41.045 --> 00:01:45.770
John earned his Bachelor's degree at Stanford and his Masters and PhD degrees at MIT.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:50.535
He joined Stanford as a professor in 1988 and over the years,

00:01:50.535 --> 00:01:53.030
has published hundreds of articles and

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.805
collaborations and taught and advised thousands of students.

00:01:56.805 --> 00:02:00.420
John's first research project in online education,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.695
started in 2009 when he and six undergraduate students built Stanford Courseware,

00:02:05.695 --> 00:02:10.430
an innovative platform that expanded to support interactive video and discussion.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:15.110
Courseware served as a foundation for initial flipped classroom experiments at

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:20.430
Stanford and helped inspire  the first massive open online courses better known as MOOCs,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.875
from Stanford, that captured worldwide attention in 2011.

00:02:24.875 --> 00:02:29.210
Since 2012, John has served as vice provost at Stanford.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.885
Most recently, as the vice provost for teaching and learning.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:34.730
Under John's leadership, SCPD transitioned

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.890
from the School of Engineering to a unit within VPTL,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.320
allowing SCPD to have a broader impact and reach across the entire university.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.025
After completing two terms as Vice Provost,

00:02:45.025 --> 00:02:47.840
John stepped down earlier this year but before

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.115
we started missing him too bad and for real,

00:02:51.115 --> 00:02:53.270
we were happy to learn that in

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.115
January he will become chair of the Computer Science Department,

00:02:56.115 --> 00:02:59.810
by far our largest collaboration in the university.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.820
So, while he's no longer my boss's boss's boss,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.415
[LAUGHTER] I'm still working for him.

00:03:05.415 --> 00:03:06.620
So, with this in mind,

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.530
please help me and give John Mitchell an SCPD welcome.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:16.615
[APPLAUSE] Thank you, John.

00:03:16.615 --> 00:03:21.415
Thank you, Segev. So, I just want to welcome you all here and

00:03:21.415 --> 00:03:26.100
congratulate you on making the excellent choice to

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.510
listen to people like me and believe what we say and

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:38.520
[LAUGHTER] go off and tell other people because it's all true and we wouldn't teach you anything if we didn't know it well here.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.680
So, I think this program here has been very successful.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.145
Many of you may not know much about the history.

00:03:46.145 --> 00:03:48.880
Apparently, in the School of Engineering,

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.355
our distance education programs date back to 1954.

00:03:53.355 --> 00:03:56.000
So, that's before I was born.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.870
I have no idea what they did in 1954,

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:03.770
[NOISE] probably something on paper in the mail or something like that.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.840
I don't know how they did distance education in 1954.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.920
I know in the 1970s,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.540
we had this kind of direct,

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.560
microwave TV link to local companies.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.044
Hewlett-Packard was one of the leading companies,

00:04:18.044 --> 00:04:20.180
in the Valley at that time.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.920
Other companies would subscribe.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.250
And I was actually a student here,

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:27.375
as Segev mentioned,

00:04:27.375 --> 00:04:29.630
some time in that decade,

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.395
and what I remember from a course that I took in this building,

00:04:33.395 --> 00:04:35.560
just right above this auditorium,

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.600
is we would all sit in the classroom and look at

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.850
our little screens and the person at the front would talk at his little screen.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.475
And whenever there was a question,

00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:49.970
somebody else in the valley could push their button at their site in Milpitas

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:54.980
or Cupertino or someplace else in Palo Alto and ask a question in real time.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.100
So, the quality of some things has gone up but we've kind of

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.580
lost that interactive and I- interaction with

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.820
you if you're remote- and I hope it'll come back some time

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.270
in the time that you're involved in this program.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.685
I'm not quite sure about that,

00:05:09.685 --> 00:05:13.570
but I really liked that interactive activity and I think that's something,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.920
we can hope for in the future.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.280
Since the 1990s, this has been a web-based delivery,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.380
which has been very successful.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.105
So, we, as Segev mentioned,

00:05:26.105 --> 00:05:28.790
offer about 200 courses a year from

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:32.540
the School of Engineering and otherwise in the format that you're

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.375
taking them and that's if you were to count

00:05:35.375 --> 00:05:39.070
the number of students enrolled on campus and where they're enrolled in.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:43.100
That's the bulk of the student course hours in the school of engineering,

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.210
are all available to all of you.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.540
So, I think that's really represents

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.414
our commitment at Stanford to reaching as many people as possible,

00:05:51.414 --> 00:05:54.045
as successfully as we can, and

00:05:54.045 --> 00:05:59.140
you all look bright and cheerful and energetic and so I'm sure we're reaching,

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.060
the right people

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:03.560
as best we can there.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.680
One thing you might not know is that you probably have

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.190
the same experience remotely as students on campus.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:17.980
It turns out that about 80 percent of the video views for the courses that we,

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:21.320
deliver in the form that you see them,

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.410
are from students on campus.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.780
So, you might not see it remotely but for most of us

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:30.350
teaching the room is empty [LAUGHTER] and everybody is remote.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:34.520
[LAUGHTER] So, you are following

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:40.980
the most popular path through Stanford [LAUGHTER] by being remote students.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.930
You're in really good company.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:46.035
I don't know if you've been to the Huang Engineering building,

00:06:46.035 --> 00:06:47.440
does anybody recognize that?

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.450
It's the place where there's a cafe

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:53.290
and stuff just over behind this building.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.000
Jen-Hsen Huang who was the donor,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.705
who is- the building is named after was a remote student.

00:06:59.705 --> 00:07:02.710
He got a master's student at Stanford and as far as I know,

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.919
he never set foot on campus,

00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:06.450
during that time.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:09.890
So, you're on the path to become

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:16.940
CEO of a company like Nvidia if you want [LAUGHTER] and if you're lucky.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:20.805
A friend of mine, Bill Dowly who used to teach in our department,

00:07:20.805 --> 00:07:22.745
has been the chief scientist

00:07:22.745 --> 00:07:25.565
of Nvidia for maybe 10 years.

00:07:25.565 --> 00:07:27.800
And [NOISE] he told me an entertaining story that I'm

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.160
not gonna repeat about Jen- Hsen Huang

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:35.710
being pulled over by the California Highway Patrol for driving his Ferrari too fast,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:37.810
down the highway.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.805
So, I wish you success.

00:07:40.805 --> 00:07:43.030
If you have that kind of success,

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:45.770
don't tell us but if you have

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:49.575
another kind of technical success around your business or

00:07:49.575 --> 00:07:51.070
your scientific career,

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.260
we love to hear about  it.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.980
The way you drive your Ferrari is kind of your business.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.675
So, please enjoy the courses.

00:07:58.675 --> 00:08:00.580
I know that they're challenging.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.210
We try to challenge everyone.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.780
That's what we do for a living.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:09.640
[LAUGHTER] But work hard, you know,

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:13.150
have fun, learn stuff and,

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:14.200
you know, keep in touch.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:18.300
You know, let us know when something is successful when something isn't.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.780
One of the things that I really appreciate very much and

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.335
it happens all too infrequently,

00:08:25.335 --> 00:08:26.770
is, you know, somebody,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.600
a year, five years,

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.340
10 years after they took a course,

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.360
will send me an email and say,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.425
you know that course I hated when I was a student?

00:08:36.425 --> 00:08:38.725
Meaning my good course, you know, [LAUGHTER]

00:08:38.725 --> 00:08:41.750
it actually was very valuable and the most important part to

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:44.860
me was this and I didn't appreciate it at the time but now I do.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.265
You know, that kind of thing is very gratifying as a teacher.

00:08:49.265 --> 00:08:51.260
We teach to, you know,

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.060
this big empty room and lots of people on video.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.300
So, hearing from you is valuable to all of

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.860
us and I know as you go forward in your careers

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:03.255
and have the success in the directions you choose, you know,

00:09:03.255 --> 00:09:04.900
your advice

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:07.610
and input to us on what we could do better or

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:11.000
what topics we should cover or how we'd been helpful to you

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.860
is very valuable to us.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.170
So please, you know,

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.560
make good connections with each other,

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.690
with other people in the courses and programs [NOISE] you enroll in and,

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:24.475
you know, keep us informed [NOISE]

00:09:24.475 --> 00:09:27.410
of what happens to you and where

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:30.195
we've been helpful to you and how we could be more,

00:09:30.195 --> 00:09:32.700
successful in reaching others.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.380
So that's basically my thought.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.800
I don't know if there are any questions but

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.105
I do appreciate all of your interest in our programs.

00:09:42.105 --> 00:09:44.180
We work hard. As Segev has said,

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.780
there's a big organization here to try to deliver to you

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:51.680
the best possible experience we can for people at a distance.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:55.655
And from a point of view of being on the faculty here,

00:09:55.655 --> 00:10:00.165
we feel very enthusiastic about our fields.

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:01.725
We love our work,

00:10:01.725 --> 00:10:03.565
we love our topic area,

00:10:03.565 --> 00:10:05.870
and we're really just very pleased to be

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:08.305
able to share that with as many people as possible.

00:10:08.305 --> 00:10:10.400
So, please have a good evening here.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.500
Enjoy meeting each other and

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:26.195
thank you for coming [APPLAUSE]

00:10:26.195 --> 00:10:26.840
Perfect.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:31.100
Thank you, John. Let me share with you the plan for the evening.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:34.230
First, I'm going to briefly overview some

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.440
of the key information you need to know as the quarter begins.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.360
Because all of this information is available online,

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.400
I'm not going to go into a lot of details about it.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:46.950
It's on our website,

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.615
the SCPD website or the university website, right?

00:10:49.615 --> 00:10:54.595
I do recommend that if anything I speak to is something that's is confusing,

00:10:54.595 --> 00:10:56.740
not familiar or new,

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:00.035
please be sure to ask questions of our team

00:11:00.035 --> 00:11:03.095
later tonight and as we go to our network receptions,

00:11:03.095 --> 00:11:06.375
we wanna make sure that you have the right information.

00:11:06.375 --> 00:11:11.030
So, after going over the short deck,

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:14.485
we're- we'll introduce our panel team members,

00:11:14.485 --> 00:11:15.760
we'll respond to your questions.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.465
Now, this is really cool and this is our first time trying it but it's been tested here.

00:11:19.465 --> 00:11:20.660
So let's give it a try.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.690
I'm going to ask you to go to pollev.com/scpd,

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:26.740
on your phone.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.780
And start,

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:30.850
you have the choice to enter an input,

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.085
a question that you'd like to ask the panel.

00:11:33.085 --> 00:11:35.265
You also have the very cool opportunity of

00:11:35.265 --> 00:11:39.408
upvoting questions that have already been submitted,

00:11:39.408 --> 00:11:41.090
and by you upvoting the question,

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:43.105
it gives us indication as to whether or not this is of

00:11:43.105 --> 00:11:45.760
interest to a larger number of individuals and

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:50.465
we'll try to go through the questions that are of most interest,

00:11:50.465 --> 00:11:51.825
to the most people,

00:11:51.825 --> 00:11:54.200
as best we can.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.830
The other cool thing about it is you can also

00:11:56.830 --> 00:12:00.290
vote down questions but we don't do this here, right?

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:02.640
[LAUGHTER] It's technically possible but it's not cool.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.425
So- so please remember that.

00:12:04.425 --> 00:12:09.535
And also our tech team is appreciating your stress testing our WiFi in the room.

00:12:09.535 --> 00:12:11.465
I hope everybody will be able to connect,

00:12:11.465 --> 00:12:14.545
doing it before the queries burden as the quarter begins.

00:12:14.545 --> 00:12:17.100
So, let's get started.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:20.580
Accessing course content, right?

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:22.005
So as we all know,

00:12:22.005 --> 00:12:24.100
our quarter begins next week.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.460
Next week is week one.

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:32.205
The most important thing for you to do is to access your course website and identify,

00:12:32.205 --> 00:12:37.370
the platform and how to access the lectures in the course.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:39.640
Go to the SCPD website,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:42.840
then click on the My Stanford connection button login,

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:45.420
and as you complete your login process, you will get to you,

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.625
essentially your dashboard where

00:12:47.625 --> 00:12:50.465
the class or classes you're taking will be listed, right?

00:12:50.465 --> 00:12:53.535
That should provide information for you at the very least,

00:12:53.535 --> 00:12:56.954
to a- to our video platform.

00:12:56.954 --> 00:13:01.720
Our video platform and video access where recorded lectures are hosted.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:05.230
Do not wait to receive a special invitation from anyone, right?

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.010
So, this is just like when you got

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.270
your undergraduate degree or any other graduate degree, right?

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.765
You're now students in university,

00:13:11.765 --> 00:13:13.760
you're expected to find your classroom,

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:15.305
even if it's a virtual classroom.

00:13:15.305 --> 00:13:18.820
Many teaching teams will send out the welcome email,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:23.120
but it's your responsibility to go and find that information.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.855
I do want to mention that depending on the course that you are enrolled in,

00:13:26.855 --> 00:13:30.970
you will access your course information either via a Canvas website.

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.270
Canvas is a Learning Management System in use here,

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:36.635
or a website, or some other platform.

00:13:36.635 --> 00:13:39.780
That information is available to you after you log in.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:43.964
For most classes that we offer here at SCPD,

00:13:43.964 --> 00:13:46.810
recorded lectures are also available in near

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:51.065
real time by logging into canvas.stanford.edu.

00:13:51.065 --> 00:13:53.410
You go to canvas.stanford.edu,

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.860
you use your student ID to sign in,

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:59.850
you get your dashboard with a tile that you can click and access your class.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:03.160
So, the nice thing about it is that because classes

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:06.450
are streamed in near real time, if you know, for example,

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:11.370
that your first class is on Monday at 9:30 and your schedule is open at work,

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.435
you can actually log into the system,

00:14:13.435 --> 00:14:16.540
and you'll be able to watch that first lecture as it is given.

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:19.290
Canvas is also the place where recording of

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:23.120
lectures and other sessions from the course will be kept throughout the quarter.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:26.440
So definitely check that out. Really easy to use.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:30.250
We get fantastic reviews for that system.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:35.920
Contacts. Any questions you have about your course, about your class,

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:39.930
the best way to get an answer is simply to contact the teaching team,

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.630
and how to contact the teaching team will be available on the class website.

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:47.885
Any other issues that are not related to the academic content or if you're not sure,

00:14:47.885 --> 00:14:52.940
please, please do not hesitate and contact our student and client services team.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:56.460
We're here to support you especially during the first week of the quarter.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.825
So, the one thing we don't want you to do is,

00:14:59.825 --> 00:15:02.260
you know, don't hesitate, right?

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.450
Ask us those questions.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:10.190
Exam Procedures. Very, very important and you'll notice the red font, right?

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:16.265
So, students in classes with in-class monitored exam must have an approved exam monitor.

00:15:16.265 --> 00:15:19.515
Information about how to get that process started

00:15:19.515 --> 00:15:23.195
is on the exam section in my Stanford connection.

00:15:23.195 --> 00:15:25.350
So just log in

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:27.140
and understand the process,

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:28.740
understand how it works.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:34.690
The autumn exam monitor nomination due deadline is Monday, October 8th.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.920
So, this is really- if there's one thing to take

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:39.290
right is to make sure you understand the process, you know,

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:44.810
who can be an exam monitor and you complete this process by October 8th.

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.530
Any delay in doing this, right?

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:50.585
Increases the likelihood of

00:15:50.585 --> 00:15:53.720
needless worrying about stuff not happening on time, right?

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:55.570
So, please

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:59.830
be sure to take that action item, that step.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:03.675
Stanford Honor Code. What is it?

00:16:03.675 --> 00:16:06.470
The Stanford Honor Code is a set of standards that

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:10.575
establish the expectation for academic integrity required of all students,

00:16:10.575 --> 00:16:13.205
and you are now students in the university, right?

00:16:13.205 --> 00:16:15.620
If you want to get- I highly recommend,

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:17.304
Google search

00:16:17.304 --> 00:16:19.810
Stanford Honor Code, you will get to university website,

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:21.890
where all the policies are detailed.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:23.795
But in a nutshell,

00:16:23.795 --> 00:16:25.630
you're supposed to do your own work.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:28.410
You're not supposed to help others with their work,

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:31.998
and others are-

00:16:31.998 --> 00:16:33.760
And vice-versa, right?

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:37.455
So- so here are some violations that I think are interesting, right?

00:16:37.455 --> 00:16:41.195
And I collected these from prior classes.

00:16:41.195 --> 00:16:45.370
The teaching team in your class will also have additions to this policy.

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:48.710
So, looking at previous years' homework solutions, right?

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.335
Either official or written by another student,

00:16:51.335 --> 00:16:53.260
discussing homework problem, right?

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:56.735
So, individual assignments are to be completed individually.

00:16:56.735 --> 00:16:59.260
Submitting malicious code, right?

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:00.675
Not an honorable thing to do,

00:17:00.675 --> 00:17:03.575
it's a violation of the honor code. Don't do it.

00:17:03.575 --> 00:17:05.590
Uploading your code,

00:17:05.590 --> 00:17:10.480
if you're dealing with computer code to a public repository like GitHub; that's a violation.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:12.320
And we have the systems in place, right?

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:19.755
So, [LAUGHTER] let's not and try and see if we have systems in place to identify these events.

00:17:19.755 --> 00:17:23.420
I will say that the process is a serious process,

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:25.334
a process of honor code violation.

00:17:25.334 --> 00:17:29.240
It is a very fair process and in the few instances where we have been involved,

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:32.390
we certainly notice that there is a

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:36.305
sincere effort on the part of the university to make sure that your side is heard.

00:17:36.305 --> 00:17:39.540
Know the policy.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:42.810
Course Evaluations. There are two opportunities for you to give feedback.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:44.985
The first one will happen midquarter.

00:17:44.985 --> 00:17:47.850
We will send to you via email the SCPD midquarter survey.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:50.280
It happens around week five.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.100
That survey is for us to understand,

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:54.960
to make sure that everything works, right?

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:58.570
It is not a vehicle for you to ask questions of the teaching team,

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:01.815
and it's not a vehicle for you to ask questions that are timely, right?

00:18:01.815 --> 00:18:06.160
We receive hundreds of responses and while we read through all those responses,

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:07.330
if there's something timely there,

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:08.730
that you need us to respond to,

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:12.030
it might take us a couple of weeks, that's not the way to go.

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:14.880
The university end of quarter evaluation

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.695
is available on the 10th and 11th week of the quarter.

00:18:17.695 --> 00:18:19.610
Very important to complete that.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.255
You do not want to

00:18:22.255 --> 00:18:23.800
cause- incompletion of it to cause

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:27.280
any delay in you getting your grades at the end of the quarter.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:30.720
Important deadlines. Important deadlines.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.930
First day of instruction is next week.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:35.220
The drop deadline is Friday,

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:40.210
October 12th, before 5:00 PM, Pacific Time.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:43.430
Withdrawals and grading basis changes,

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:46.825
November 16; incomplete request, December seven.

00:18:46.825 --> 00:18:50.230
Assignments and exam are subject to your class syllabus, right?

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:54.905
So, [NOISE] you're all working professionals, most of you are.

00:18:54.905 --> 00:18:57.740
However, work-related travel

00:18:57.740 --> 00:18:59.700
or heavy workload

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.620
generally speaking is not a-

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:06.530
is not considered a sufficient justification for skipping a deadline, right?

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:08.390
So, at the same time,

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:10.040
we know that life happens.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:13.925
When events outside your control happen

00:19:13.925 --> 00:19:16.120
that might cause you to miss a deadline,

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:20.905
[NOISE] be diligent and inform the teaching teams as early as you can,

00:19:20.905 --> 00:19:25.095
and do not hesitate to involve the Student Client Services team also.

00:19:25.095 --> 00:19:29.560
The earlier we know of a challenge the earlier we have an opportunity to help.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:31.570
The last one, the last item here I

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:33.290
want to speak to is tuition reimbursement, right?

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:36.205
Many of you will receive tuition reimbursement from your companies.

00:19:36.205 --> 00:19:39.360
Some companies because autumn quarter ends,

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.700
very near to the end of December,

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:45.340
end of the year. Some companies stop processing these requests,

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:47.675
say on December 15th, right?

00:19:47.675 --> 00:19:51.825
Your responsibility is to understand the policy at your company.

00:19:51.825 --> 00:19:53.840
Know if you have any deadlines

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:57.035
for submitting tuition reimbursement request in your company,

00:19:57.035 --> 00:19:58.780
and make sure that, you know,

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:00.800
when will the grades be available.

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:02.675
In certain cases

00:20:02.675 --> 00:20:05.580
we can help you get your grades a few days earlier,

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:08.540
but please, let us know about that in advance.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.170
These are the important policies, right?

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:15.740
[NOISE] All of it is on the website.

