But maybe if you want to build on that,

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:01.865
have either of you had that opportunity to reach out and

00:28:01.865 --> 00:28:04.440
make connections with other students at SCPD?

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:06.485
And if so, could you talk about that?

00:28:06.485 --> 00:28:10.150
Yeah, I stay in conversation with quite a few SCPD students,

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:12.375
especially those who are in the HCP program.

00:28:12.375 --> 00:28:14.875
So, one of the things that you guys will probably

00:28:14.875 --> 00:28:18.420
experience is a lot of the SCPD students like to use what's called Slack.

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:20.200
It's a QuickTime messaging system,

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:21.735
kind of like Facebook Messenger.

00:28:21.735 --> 00:28:25.460
So, I was actually just chatting with another SCPD student before this through Slack,

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:29.320
and we use that avenue Piazza like she was mentioning before,

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:32.710
and Skype or video chat.

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:34.430
So, that's my experience with it.

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:37.230
Yeah,

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:41.170
there's a Slack that I think a lot of SCPD students are on and then, there's also,

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:44.420
just if you- depending on what companies you work on,

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:47.005
you might find that there are some people in your company already taking,

00:28:47.005 --> 00:28:48.795
courses through SCPD.

00:28:48.795 --> 00:28:50.190
That might be how you were introduced to it,

00:28:50.190 --> 00:28:52.745
so you can reach out to them, and also,

00:28:52.745 --> 00:28:57.360
you can just use Piazza or so on to meet up with other SCPD students.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:01.430
Fantastic. And Tushar, maybe hold on to the microphone and just could you share,

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:04.885
so Zara and Nick offered their sort of high-level feedback.

00:29:04.885 --> 00:29:06.830
What would be your insights,

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:09.955
if you think back to your first quarter here with SCPD?

00:29:09.955 --> 00:29:13.190
I mean, what course were you taking, and you know,

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:17.510
what do you wish you had known before you began in the program?

00:29:17.510 --> 00:29:22.145
Yeah, so my first quarter with SCPD, I took,

00:29:22.145 --> 00:29:24.575
I can't remember the exact course number, but it was client-side,

00:29:24.575 --> 00:29:27.345
web programming essentially.

00:29:27.345 --> 00:29:29.750
And it was really good.

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:30.920
I actually think

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:32.045
for your first course,

00:29:32.045 --> 00:29:34.175
you might want to consider taking,

00:29:34.175 --> 00:29:36.140
it was a lighter course, it was a 100-level course.

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.060
And so, you might want to consider taking something like that to kind of

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.405
gauge the workload and so on.

00:29:40.405 --> 00:29:42.390
So, I

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:44.140
I think that was really helpful not to start with,

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:45.620
you know, a heavier course.

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:48.210
And just if I were to go back,

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:49.960
one of the things would be, you know, in general,

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:53.590
you got to- you have to think of all these courses and your SCPD kind

00:29:53.590 --> 00:29:57.255
of career as more of a marathon rather than a sprint.

00:29:57.255 --> 00:30:02.765
So, you know, try to- if you take all the intense courses all at once, you know,

00:30:02.765 --> 00:30:07.255
or try to take multiple courses in your first quarter or anything like that,

00:30:07.255 --> 00:30:09.940
you might, you know, get turned off from the entire program.

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:11.320
So, definitely pace yourself,

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:14.510
take breaks in between quarters if you need it, and you know,

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:16.730
just think about it as being,

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:20.140
a longer-term investment rather than just one-quarter investment.

00:30:20.140 --> 00:30:21.760
That's great. Colby, do you have,

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:23.480
anything that you would add to that?

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:26.800
No, no. I, I agree with him completely about the longer-term investment.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:29.160
These courses are very intense and the

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.220
teaching team puts a lot of effort into

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:33.500
making sure that it's really a unique experience for you.

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.040
So, you're not just going to be doing problems out of

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:37.030
the back of the book, you'll be very engaged.

00:30:37.030 --> 00:30:38.740
So, I mean, I agree with him completely.

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:40.155
It is a marathon.

00:30:40.155 --> 00:30:44.910
Could you both maybe talk a little bit more about the kind of workload that you,

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:47.575
experienced and what your typical week like,

00:30:47.575 --> 00:30:49.310
is like as an SCPD student?

00:30:49.310 --> 00:30:53.040
Yes, so I'm taking one class or I'm fixing to start up one class right now,

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:54.790
but, I take one class a quarter.

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:58.685
So, my workload, I dedicate probably about three days a week towards,

00:30:58.685 --> 00:31:00.835
you know, just this class in the evenings or so.

00:31:00.835 --> 00:31:02.644
That means attending

00:31:02.644 --> 00:31:06.460
the teaching assistants' live Q and A sessions, that you can ask.

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:08.580
it's typically through Skype or some other method,

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:11.570
and engaging with other SCPD students.

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:13.865
I've actually been on campus quite a few times.

00:31:13.865 --> 00:31:15.490
For my last class,

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:16.930
I did a presentation here,

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:18.170
for my first class here.

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:20.720
You know, I spent an entire month down here working

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:22.560
on a robotics competition.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.800
So, my workload is typically pretty heavy with SCPD.

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:28.990
I'd say 20 to 30 hours a week is average,

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:31.515
sometimes heavier, depending on what classes you're taking.

00:31:31.515 --> 00:31:33.020
Okay.

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:35.245
Yeah, I would say

00:31:35.245 --> 00:31:37.610
it's very dependent on the class you take.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:39.169
I've had classes where there was

00:31:39.169 --> 00:31:41.690
15 hours a week and I've had classes where I was above,

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:43.365
30 hours a week.

00:31:43.365 --> 00:31:46.510
The key thing I think,

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:49.030
which was pointed out earlier is having a study group

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:51.855
probably like cuts your workload down significantly.

00:31:51.855 --> 00:31:52.980
I think my first few classes,

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:54.710
I just tried to go it alone and you know,

00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:55.765
just do it all by myself,

00:31:55.765 --> 00:31:59.280
and that probably added like ten hours a week to my workload.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:01.610
But it's

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:04.840
an investment of time especially if you're working.

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:07.655
And the other thing is, if you

00:32:07.655 --> 00:32:10.695
want to take classes,

00:32:10.695 --> 00:32:12.720
almost everybody I know is taking one class a quarter.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:15.810
So, if you're considering taking more than one class a quarter,

00:32:15.810 --> 00:32:17.710
definitely like really strongly consider

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:20.275
that decision and make sure you know what you're getting into before you do that.

00:32:20.275 --> 00:32:21.255
That's great advice.

00:32:21.255 --> 00:32:22.930
I did two classes it sucked,

00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:26.375
so [LAUGHTER] I was here until like 3:00 AM for like multiple times a week.

00:32:26.375 --> 00:32:28.805
So, yeah. So, I advise against it.

00:32:28.805 --> 00:32:29.875
Heard it here, you heard it here.

00:32:29.875 --> 00:32:30.340
Yeah.

00:32:30.340 --> 00:32:31.930
So maybe building on that, Zara,

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:34.855
could you talk about some of the common difficulties and struggles that you

00:32:34.855 --> 00:32:37.930
see SCPD students facing on your side?

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:41.840
Oh, yeah, sure. I think one of the most common problem is that

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:46.015
the SCPD student start working on an assignments too late.

00:32:46.015 --> 00:32:49.935
Start working on an assignment as soon as it is published and posted.

00:32:49.935 --> 00:32:54.495
Because if you just postpone doing the assignments on the weekend,

00:32:54.495 --> 00:32:56.575
that's not how it works, right?

00:32:56.575 --> 00:32:59.520
So, on weekends you see some problem, you post on Piazza,

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:01.640
it takes some time for the teaching

00:33:01.640 --> 00:33:03.835
staff because they are on the weekends too, right?

00:33:03.835 --> 00:33:06.230
So, you should expect some delays.

00:33:06.230 --> 00:33:08.700
And then, you would, I mean,

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:11.570
there are other SCPD might complain that "Hey,

00:33:11.570 --> 00:33:13.775
it's like, let's say,

00:33:13.775 --> 00:33:16.190
five hours that I posted my question,

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:18.315
why I didn't get my- any answer yet?"

00:33:18.315 --> 00:33:20.410
So, try to

00:33:20.410 --> 00:33:24.495
start working on the assignment as early as it is published.

00:33:24.495 --> 00:33:27.385
And then the other thing,

00:33:27.385 --> 00:33:29.430
as they mentioned,

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:32.300
so I was TA for 229 and 230.

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:36.825
Also I took 221 and other CS courses.

00:33:36.825 --> 00:33:38.815
They're not easy courses.

00:33:38.815 --> 00:33:42.425
They are intense. Be prepared for them.

00:33:42.425 --> 00:33:45.825
So, we had like a ton of emails

00:33:45.825 --> 00:33:49.005
like three weeks before finals, saying that, "Hey,

00:33:49.005 --> 00:33:52.710
I didn't expect this workload from these courses.

00:33:52.710 --> 00:33:55.235
May I request incomplete?

00:33:55.235 --> 00:33:57.765
May I drop the course?"

00:33:57.765 --> 00:34:01.940
This is not something that usually happen.

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:04.840
So, if you think you cannot

00:34:04.840 --> 00:34:07.835
dedicate enough time to this course,

00:34:07.835 --> 00:34:13.195
just reconsider your decision for taking this course.

00:34:13.195 --> 00:34:15.200
We have, as far as I know,

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:20.225
we have a long wait list for the students wants to take this course and,

00:34:20.225 --> 00:34:22.570
they can't be enrolled.

00:34:22.570 --> 00:34:28.800
So, if you think you cannot dedicate enough time for these intense courses,

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:32.040
reconsider your decision.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:33.460
Thank you.

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:35.055
Tushar did you wanna-

00:34:35.055 --> 00:34:36.665
I'm sorry.

00:34:36.665 --> 00:34:38.310
Just so it's not all, you know,

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:39.400
[LAUGHTER] just really doom and gloom.

00:34:39.400 --> 00:34:41.495
[LAUGHTER] I think there's definitely, like,

00:34:41.495 --> 00:34:45.680
a rhythm and it might take you til like your second or third quarter to hit that rhythm.

00:34:45.680 --> 00:34:47.275
But, you know, your first quarter you're going to

00:34:47.275 --> 00:34:48.940
be trying to figure out how to balance like,

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:50.620
you know, what time do I watch the lectures on.

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:52.560
Do I watch it after dinner? Do I watch on the weekends?

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:54.330
And you're going to be trying to figure out how like, you know,

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.695
should I start the assignments early or should I wait till,

00:34:56.695 --> 00:34:58.560
you know, later or how long does it take?

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.730
By the time I maybe hit my third course or my fourth course,

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:03.130
I was starting to get into this rhythm,

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:04.590
and when you get into that rhythm,

00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:05.680
like, it makes it a lot easier.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:07.330
Because you know that, okay the lecture is

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:09.530
published Tuesday I have to set aside some time to,

00:35:09.530 --> 00:35:10.770
like, watch it Tuesday night.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:11.980
I'm gonna, like, do the assignment.

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:13.470
I have friends here that I'm going to contact.

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:15.995
I'm gonna, like, pose my question on Piazza early, like,

00:35:15.995 --> 00:35:17.660
It is very,

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:19.520
very hard not to understate it at all,

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:21.620
but don't be completely scared off from it.

00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:23.660
You'll get the hang of it eventually.

00:35:23.660 --> 00:35:27.115
Nick, do you want to add anything common struggles that you see?

00:35:27.115 --> 00:35:29.820
Yeah, sure. So I think, you know,

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.470
one of the things that we try to encourage a lot,

00:35:32.470 --> 00:35:34.300
to help people do really well in the classes is to take

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:36.545
advantage of all of the resources that we publish.

00:35:36.545 --> 00:35:40.670
And what I mean by that is a lot of the extra session videos that we record,

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:43.570
a lot of the additional handouts and section problems that we post,

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:47.290
as one of the remote students that John Mitchell mentioned.

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:48.730
Even though I was an on-campus student,

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:50.770
I know a lot of the struggles around, you know,

00:35:50.770 --> 00:35:52.810
keeping yourself motivated to keep up with the material,

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:54.025
especially if you're not attending

00:35:54.025 --> 00:35:56.400
lecture on a regular basis. [NOISE] Cough, cough.

00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:58.260
As I did, you know,

00:35:58.260 --> 00:36:00.300
regrettably, whatever, I graduated.

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:02.510
So it's all fine. [LAUGHTER].

00:36:02.510 --> 00:36:04.095
But the point being, you know,

00:36:04.095 --> 00:36:05.930
like, in, you know,

00:36:05.930 --> 00:36:08.600
one of our classes CS 106A or 106B, you know,

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:11.140
we have discussion sections every week that are required for

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:14.115
on-campus students but are obviously optional for remote students.

00:36:14.115 --> 00:36:16.220
We make videos available for those and,

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:18.370
and a lot of times, from feedback from on-campus students,

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:20.110
we know that these discussion sections are one of

00:36:20.110 --> 00:36:23.130
the single most helpful resources that we have for students because,

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:24.450
especially in computer science,

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:27.005
practice is what makes you better at the subject.

00:36:27.005 --> 00:36:30.290
So, even if videos like that are published and you're not

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:34.370
required to attend a section or watch a section live streamed or anything like that,

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:36.580
we highly recommend that you, you know,

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:39.250
pretend that you're in a discussion section at a specific time,

00:36:39.250 --> 00:36:41.070
or watch the video as soon as it's published,

00:36:41.070 --> 00:36:42.230
or, you know,

00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:44.500
take advantage of all those resources even if there's not

00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:48.430
a structured reinforced requirement for you to use them.

00:36:48.430 --> 00:36:50.210
You know, we try to post them for a reason.

00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:53.725
We post them when we see needs that students raise for,

00:36:53.725 --> 00:36:55.480
you know, I didn't understand this well enough.

00:36:55.480 --> 00:36:58.565
Okay, well, let's try to post an additional video clarifying that topic.

00:36:58.565 --> 00:37:01.605
You know, a lot of times we see people, you know,

00:37:01.605 --> 00:37:03.575
right before exams or assignments are due,

00:37:03.575 --> 00:37:05.220
you know, they'll try to binge all the videos.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:08.260
And, you know, I know firsthand it can work maybe sometimes,

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:11.110
[LAUGHTER] but, you know a lot of the times, you know,

00:37:11.110 --> 00:37:14.320
we try to structure the course in the schedule and post resources in

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:17.560
a way that if you can keep up with the schedule as much as possible,

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:19.775
you have a great chance of being successful.

00:37:19.775 --> 00:37:23.435
Nick, can you- so you mentioned some of the discussion sections.

00:37:23.435 --> 00:37:26.240
How does office hours- I know it probably depends on the course,

00:37:26.240 --> 00:37:28.250
but how does office hours work for remote students?

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:30.375
Sure. So, a lot of times,

00:37:30.375 --> 00:37:31.960
you know, at least in the experience that I've had,

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:33.025
we try to hold

00:37:33.025 --> 00:37:35.400
remote office hours over Google Hangouts or Queue Status,

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:37.230
if any of you have experienced with Queue Status,

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:38.960
where you can sign up online.

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:43.090
We'll video call you and we'll help you one-on-one on your computer remotely.

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:45.570
Just tying back to one one the things I said earlier,

00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:49.610
a lot of the time when we're trying to schedule office hours for students,

00:37:49.610 --> 00:37:53.590
we are eager to hear from you about how we can improve office hours times,

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:55.565
structure, the way that we organize,

00:37:55.565 --> 00:37:57.290
you know, how we help students, you know,

00:37:57.290 --> 00:37:58.970
whether it's, this time isn't great for me,

00:37:58.970 --> 00:38:02.030
this time would be better or I had an experience where, you know,

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:03.670
something, you know,

00:38:03.670 --> 00:38:05.890
a TA did this and I wish they had done that.

00:38:05.890 --> 00:38:08.430
We love that kind of feedback to improve our office hours

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:11.050
because you're the ones that are taking advantage of them, you know.

00:38:11.050 --> 00:38:12.750
We don't have on-campus students that are taking

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:15.495
advantage of those remote office hours. Those are just for you.

00:38:15.495 --> 00:38:17.450
But it really does depend on the class.

00:38:17.450 --> 00:38:21.600
Some classes will combine remote and local office hours at the same time.

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:25.759
Others will have discrete office hours for SCPD students alone.

00:38:25.759 --> 00:38:28.050
So it really depends on the course structure, but they,

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:31.715
will usually are always eager to hear feedback for how they can make that better.

00:38:31.715 --> 00:38:34.135
Thank you. Zahra, do you wanna add anything to that?

00:38:34.135 --> 00:38:36.820
Yeah, sure. Specifically for, ah,

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:38.740
machine learning course CS 229,

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:40.400
we're gonna have, like,

00:38:40.400 --> 00:38:42.660
a post on Piazza and asking,

00:38:42.660 --> 00:38:48.405
when is the most convenient time for you SCPD students to attend office hours.

00:38:48.405 --> 00:38:50.080
Is it, like, after 05:00 PM,

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:51.695
or is it early morning?

00:38:51.695 --> 00:38:54.985
Depends on what time zone you are.

00:38:54.985 --> 00:39:00.460
And then we're gonna pick up the most convenient time that satisfy, you know,

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:04.075
it cannot satisfy the needs of all of the SCPD students,

00:39:04.075 --> 00:39:06.450
but at least we can try to

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:09.635
satisfy most of them. Yeah.

00:39:09.635 --> 00:39:12.120
Great, thanks. And actually sticking with you Zahra,

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:13.305
so we've gotten a few questions here.

00:39:13.305 --> 00:39:15.840
You've been a TA for some of the really big courses,

00:39:15.840 --> 00:39:19.220
can you maybe help give the students some of the insight into how that teaching team works,

00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:21.645
because it's a big teaching team and so just they

00:39:21.645 --> 00:39:22.900
can have

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:24.010
some kind of sympathy to,

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:26.730
the structure of your team and how it works.

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:29.835
Thank you. Last year we have

00:39:29.835 --> 00:39:33.330
over 1200 students

00:39:33.330 --> 00:39:35.710
for CS 2229, I guess.

00:39:35.710 --> 00:39:38.410
Like 600 students for CS 230.

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:46.740
So, one thing that I want to mention that remember that TAs or teaching staff,

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:48.485
they are grad students.

00:39:48.485 --> 00:39:52.800
So, they're not full-time employees sitting back and

00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:57.600
behind it- their laptops answering your question on Piazza or on emails.

00:39:57.600 --> 00:40:00.435
They need to take some courses.

00:40:00.435 --> 00:40:03.535
They also have some TA's answering their questions.

00:40:03.535 --> 00:40:06.395
So, maybe, it will be nice if

00:40:06.395 --> 00:40:10.020
you give some time for them to get back to your questions,

00:40:10.020 --> 00:40:12.810
some decent time, some reasonable time.

00:40:12.810 --> 00:40:17.860
And one specific advice for the ones are taking 229, 221,

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:20.840
or 230 is that, like,

00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:23.235
four weeks after the quarter starts,

00:40:23.235 --> 00:40:26.620
we're gonna tell you who is your project TA is.

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:30.925
Make sure that you know who's your project TA is because the project TA is the

00:40:30.925 --> 00:40:35.480
one that's gonna evaluate your project at the end of the quarter.

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:39.590
So, um, you will be in contact with your project

00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:44.050
TA and also make sure that you get the course website.

00:40:44.050 --> 00:40:47.885
Make sure that we have a specific time slot for the SCPDs.

00:40:47.885 --> 00:40:51.405
So make sure that you know where this time slot is.

00:40:51.405 --> 00:40:54.785
We either, we mostly use Zoom,

00:40:54.785 --> 00:40:58.015
for communicating with the SCPD students.

00:40:58.015 --> 00:41:01.040
Make sure that you know how Zoom works,

00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:04.810
what's the ID for the TAs,

00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:08.950
and then make sure that you're online at the same time that they are online.

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:14.080
So, basically the course website and the Piazza

00:41:14.080 --> 00:41:15.910
are two main

00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:19.405
sources that you should prefer.

00:41:19.405 --> 00:41:25.840
One more advice that I can give you is that always be on top of your grades.

00:41:25.840 --> 00:41:29.515
So basically, we try to

00:41:29.515 --> 00:41:33.865
upload your grades for each assignment straight

00:41:33.865 --> 00:41:35.725
after you submit your assignment.

00:41:35.725 --> 00:41:37.920
We gonna have like a week to grade you,

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:39.530
and then we publish your grade.

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:41.515
Make sure that you see your grade,

00:41:41.515 --> 00:41:43.415
and if you have any complaint,

00:41:43.415 --> 00:41:48.740
make sure that you- we gonna give you like a week for the submitting your complaint.

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:52.700
Make sure that you submit your complaint in that specific window.

00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:57.245
Do not postpone all of your complaint by end of the quarter, right.

00:41:57.245 --> 00:41:58.995
That's not how it works.

00:41:58.995 --> 00:42:02.010
We have, like, a ton of requests at the end of the quarter,

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:07.620
and it is in the best of your benefit to know what your grade is for each assignment,

00:42:07.620 --> 00:42:11.160
and if you have any complaint just submit it right away.

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:12.515
Okay. Thank you, Zahra.

00:42:12.515 --> 00:42:12.955
Sure.

00:42:12.955 --> 00:42:14.920
Colby, Tushar, maybe, sort of,

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:17.010
building on what you were talking about earlier,

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:18.525
we like this idea of creating a rhythm.

00:42:18.525 --> 00:42:20.540
What were some of the ways, you know,

00:42:20.540 --> 00:42:22.800
that you've gotten help from your boss,

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:24.760
or from family members, or from friends?

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:28.205
How have you reached out to them to help support you to, I mean,

00:42:28.205 --> 00:42:30.040
to accomplish at 20, you know,

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:32.460
essentially a part-time job on top of your full-time?

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:35.860
Well, my supervisor was my exam proctor most of the time,

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:38.470
so he was fully aware of what my course load was,

00:42:38.470 --> 00:42:41.300
and there was a time where we were working seven days a week.

00:42:41.300 --> 00:42:45.540
So, of course, I had to take some time out of work to actually complete this coursework,

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:47.070
so just keeping them engaged.

00:42:47.070 --> 00:42:48.320
And I'm married, as well.

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:50.830
So, when I was traveling quite a bit,

00:42:50.830 --> 00:42:53.330
my wife was always like, "You're just traveling all the time.

00:42:53.330 --> 00:42:56.095
Now you're doing schoolwork. We don't really get a lot of time together."

00:42:56.095 --> 00:42:59.590
So, you know, it's just about having that open communication and,

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:01.980
you know, kind of, planning your actual schedule.

00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:04.785
So some of the things I did to plan the schedule were you know.

00:43:04.785 --> 00:43:08.410
Talk to the teaching assistants how long should this homework be taking.

00:43:08.410 --> 00:43:11.905
If I'm extending it beyond this time go looking for help or something and,

00:43:11.905 --> 00:43:14.200
you know, just plan your day around that, I guess.

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:14.680
Okay.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:23.845
[LAUGHTER].

00:43:23.845 --> 00:43:25.555
Yeah, to build on that,

00:43:25.555 --> 00:43:28.440
communication is obviously very, very important, like,

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:30.750
make sure your boss knows what [LAUGHTER] you're doing,

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:32.425
of course,

00:43:32.425 --> 00:43:35.200
and, you know, it's gonna take some time.

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:35.840
I think, like,

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:37.560
one of the things is that the start of every course you're

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:39.290
gonna get a syllabus and it'll have, you know,

00:43:39.290 --> 00:43:40.880
here are all the important dates,

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:43.941
here are the exam dates, here are the assignment dates.

00:43:43.941 --> 00:43:47.210
I throw all the exam dates and stuff into my calendar just so, you know,

00:43:47.210 --> 00:43:49.305
there's no surprises later on, you know,

00:43:49.305 --> 00:43:51.340
your manager hopefully can see

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:54.280
your calendar and they know that you're possibly booked for that time.

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:56.325
There's also,

00:43:56.325 --> 00:43:58.370
I think, one important

00:43:58.370 --> 00:44:00.320
piece of advice I got when I started was,

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:03.220
you know, despite your best efforts,

00:44:03.220 --> 00:44:05.340
despite your boss's best efforts and, you know,

00:44:05.340 --> 00:44:07.020
the core staff's best efforts,

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:08.720
there might be times where it's gonna be like,

00:44:08.720 --> 00:44:10.095
I had this work deadline,

00:44:10.095 --> 00:44:11.585
and I have this,

00:44:11.585 --> 00:44:13.610
you know, school deadline and they conflict,

00:44:13.610 --> 00:44:16.750
and there's just not much anybody can do,

00:44:16.750 --> 00:44:18.280
and like you planned in advance and everything,

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:19.290
and that's gonna happen.

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:23.380
And one of the things about finding your rhythm is just realizing that- that happens.

00:44:23.380 --> 00:44:25.660
It's part of the whole SCPD thing,

00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:27.155
instead of being a full-time student.

00:44:27.155 --> 00:44:30.460
And so if that happens don't beat yourself up over it,

00:44:30.460 --> 00:44:32.650
prioritize like you would prioritize anything else, you know,

00:44:32.650 --> 00:44:35.420
maybe you don't finish every single question on the assignment, right?

00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:38.685
You just finish the first five or the five that you knew or something.

00:44:38.685 --> 00:44:43.815
And, yeah, just talk to the core staff and use your late days, so, yeah.

00:44:43.815 --> 00:44:46.370
We've gotten a couple questions about exam monitors.

00:44:46.370 --> 00:44:47.560
I know you can't answer all of those,

00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:50.085
but can you maybe just talk briefly about your experience,

00:44:50.085 --> 00:44:53.115
with doing- taking exams as an SCPD student.

00:44:53.115 --> 00:44:56.770
Yeah. So, I had to designate an exam monitor so I

00:44:56.770 --> 00:45:00.230
just chose my manager but I've never- I've always taken my exams on campus.

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:02.270
So, I just come and take the exam,

00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:04.420
I've taken the exam in this room many times,

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:06.750
in some other rooms and you just take it with

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.235
the students that are taking it here,

00:45:08.235 --> 00:45:11.270
if you live in the area.

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:12.660
I guess maybe you can say something to the monitor. [LAUGHTER]

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:14.480
Yeah. So, I use my supervisor.

00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:19.110
I was in Australia when I first started here in 2016 and, yeah,

00:45:19.110 --> 00:45:21.530
so, I think there's a list on the SCPD website

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:24.115
that tells you who you can nominate as an exam monitor.

00:45:24.115 --> 00:45:26.170
One of those are your direct supervisors,

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:28.100
the other ones are like testing centers,

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:30.320
and I think a library is in there or something like that,

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:32.505
but, yeah, I mean.

00:45:32.505 --> 00:45:34.390
So, I use my supervisor,

00:45:34.390 --> 00:45:35.675
it worked out really well.

00:45:35.675 --> 00:45:37.240
It's just engaging with him,

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:39.540
letting him know when all the exam dates were and always

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:42.535
blocking out time on his and your calendar to,

00:45:42.535 --> 00:45:45.050
you know, have a plan to actually take the exam that day,

00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:47.580
but I never had any issues with it I guess.

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:48.420
Okay.

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:53.385
Zahra and Nick sort of keeping the questions on exams.

00:45:53.385 --> 00:45:55.290
How do you- how would you recommend that students

00:45:55.290 --> 00:45:57.760
prepare for those kind of big deadlines in the quarter,

00:45:57.760 --> 00:46:01.180
the midterms, the finals or big final projects?

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:06.715
Sure. So, start early, again.

00:46:06.715 --> 00:46:11.425
Do not- so make sure that you know when exactly is the midterm,

00:46:11.425 --> 00:46:15.175
specifically for 229, 221, and 230.

00:46:15.175 --> 00:46:20.490
And then, try to start preparing for the exams,

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:23.400
like two weeks ahead of the exam.

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:27.140
The exams are not, again, easy.

00:46:27.140 --> 00:46:33.200
And then you're competing with the most brilliant students in the world, in Stanford.

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:35.855
They are taking the same course as you're taking,

00:46:35.855 --> 00:46:40.385
and then we are looking at the distribution of the grades at the end straight.

00:46:40.385 --> 00:46:48.095
So, make sure that you start preparing for the exams early in the quarter, and

00:46:48.095 --> 00:46:52.720
we post midterm questions,

00:46:52.720 --> 00:46:55.065
like, a week before the exam,

00:46:55.065 --> 00:46:58.170
make sure that you went through that practice midterms.

00:46:58.170 --> 00:47:03.955
And make sure that you know what you're expected in the exam time.

00:47:03.955 --> 00:47:09.185
Also, we post the logistics of the exams like two weeks before the exams.

00:47:09.185 --> 00:47:11.035
Make sure that on the Piazza,

00:47:11.035 --> 00:47:12.965
make sure that you read all of them,

00:47:12.965 --> 00:47:15.970
and there's no surprises on the exam time.

00:47:15.970 --> 00:47:18.145
Okay. Nick, do you have anything?

00:47:18.145 --> 00:47:19.800
Sure, yeah. So, I think

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:21.760
there are a couple of things that I've seen over time.

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:23.970
The first one is, you know,

00:47:23.970 --> 00:47:26.945
a lot of classes have review sessions outside of their normal class time,

00:47:26.945 --> 00:47:28.610
and so for that reason, you know,

00:47:28.610 --> 00:47:30.390
it's not something that everyone can attend, you know,

00:47:30.390 --> 00:47:31.930
whether you're on campus or where you're remote,

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:33.580
you know, you can watch an additional video.

00:47:33.580 --> 00:47:35.850
But, one way or another, I'd highly recommend

00:47:35.850 --> 00:47:38.400
taking the time that the course provides you to, kind of,

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:41.730
reflect and rehash all the material that you've already learned and take that chance

00:47:41.730 --> 00:47:45.150
to reach out to the core staff if you have any confusion,

00:47:45.150 --> 00:47:47.950
or, you know, misunderstandings about any of the course material.

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:51.930
We do our best to try to mark a period of time in between,

00:47:51.930 --> 00:47:53.775
you know, assignments and the midterm,

00:47:53.775 --> 00:47:57.660
where we are eager to answer as many questions as we can and say,

00:47:57.660 --> 00:47:59.560
"Hey try these practice problems,

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:02.145
tell us if you have any questions or are confused."

00:48:02.145 --> 00:48:04.230
And along those similar lines,

00:48:04.230 --> 00:48:07.190
try to form a study group either online or in person.

00:48:07.190 --> 00:48:09.750
I know, you know, from personal experience study groups

00:48:09.750 --> 00:48:12.090
are like the bomb because everyone's like, "Oh,

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:13.655
you know that half, I know this half,

00:48:13.655 --> 00:48:14.840
I love you", you know,

00:48:14.840 --> 00:48:16.460
and you can study together, [LAUGHTER] you know,

00:48:16.460 --> 00:48:18.505
and then it'll all work out, except I'm not condoning cheating.

00:48:18.505 --> 00:48:19.840
No, I'm not. [LAUGHTER]

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:21.115
But the point is that, you know,

00:48:21.115 --> 00:48:23.470
if you get together with somebody who's really solid on

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:26.565
one subject and you're really solid in another and you can help each other out,

00:48:26.565 --> 00:48:27.930
whether it's meeting in person,

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:29.190
and doing problems together,

00:48:29.190 --> 00:48:30.980
or even something I've done in the past,

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:34.080
which is teaming up together and making an online study guide together and saying.

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:35.790
"Hey why don't you try to fill in this part,

00:48:35.790 --> 00:48:38.995
I'll fill in this part and then we can go over and discuss the material together."

00:48:38.995 --> 00:48:42.810
So, having somebody to bounce ideas and challenges

00:48:42.810 --> 00:48:46.550
and questions off of whether it's the teaching staff or another fellow student,

00:48:46.550 --> 00:48:48.560
I think is really helpful come exam time.

00:48:48.560 --> 00:48:50.960
Fantastic. We're nearing the end and so,

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:53.710
I actually just some of the tips that you've provided I was

00:48:53.710 --> 00:48:56.764
thinking maybe as a kind of a final takeaway.

00:48:56.764 --> 00:48:58.970
What would be, especially in these first two weeks,

00:48:58.970 --> 00:49:01.180
where would be the one or two things that you would recommend

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:04.300
all students do in those first two weeks?

00:49:04.300 --> 00:49:07.015
I think the number one thing I recommend

00:49:07.015 --> 00:49:10.135
is talk to your professor and your teaching staff,

00:49:10.135 --> 00:49:12.310
just as a person, not in an academic setting.

00:49:12.310 --> 00:49:14.140
Just introduce yourself, your background,

00:49:14.140 --> 00:49:16.415
what you're interested in, why you're here, why you're doing this.

00:49:16.415 --> 00:49:17.975
I think, you know,

00:49:17.975 --> 00:49:20.540
a lot of things make you as a crowd unique but one of

00:49:20.540 --> 00:49:24.360
the most interesting things is a lot of you are doing this in a professional setting,

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:26.665
you're doing this to achieve professional goals,

00:49:26.665 --> 00:49:28.230
or do more in your workplace,

00:49:28.230 --> 00:49:30.995
or work on projects that you couldn't previously do before.

00:49:30.995 --> 00:49:33.140
We love to hear about that, you know.

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:34.760
It's different when you're a student, obviously,

00:49:34.760 --> 00:49:36.885
you're a student working towards a full-time degree,

00:49:36.885 --> 00:49:39.360
and you obviously have a lot of interest as well, but, you know,

00:49:39.360 --> 00:49:42.750
you as a crowd, are in a unique position where in a lot of cases you're

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:46.985
applying what you learn directly in a job that you're doing at the same time?

00:49:46.985 --> 00:49:50.290
And there's nothing that makes us more excited than hearing about.

00:49:50.290 --> 00:49:52.500
"Oh, you know, I learned about this in your lecture last week,

00:49:52.500 --> 00:49:53.780
and then this week, like, you know,

00:49:53.780 --> 00:49:55.240
we were working on a project and,

00:49:55.240 --> 00:49:57.330
you know, I got to apply what I learned."

00:49:57.330 --> 00:50:02.150
Or, you know, if you have an open-ended project in a class and you decide to, you know,

00:50:02.150 --> 00:50:04.200
incorporate some aspect of your work in

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:06.860
that project and have kinda hit two birds with one stone,

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:09.090
that is fantastic and we love to hear about that.

00:50:09.090 --> 00:50:10.985
So, you know, it really makes us excited,

00:50:10.985 --> 00:50:12.090
it lets us connect with you,

00:50:12.090 --> 00:50:13.670
so I'd highly recommend you do that.

00:50:13.670 --> 00:50:16.360
Do you have any other examples of sort of the key contributions,

00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:19.680
the way an SCPD student has really added to or increased

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:23.575
the class experience for themselves and other students?

00:50:23.575 --> 00:50:25.370
Yeah. I mean, we, I mean obviously,

00:50:25.370 --> 00:50:27.900
you all have a really unique background where in a lot of

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:30.960
cases you have more experience either from,

00:50:30.960 --> 00:50:33.650
you know, past degree standpoint or an industry standpoint,

00:50:33.650 --> 00:50:37.065
where, you know, we've had Piazza discussion groups as others had mentioned.

00:50:37.065 --> 00:50:39.770
You know, where we've seen SCPD students chime in with like,

00:50:39.770 --> 00:50:42.160
"Oh well in this background that I had from a previous degree

00:50:42.160 --> 00:50:44.635
I did or this job that I'm doing or something like that."

00:50:44.635 --> 00:50:47.700
They'll chime in with this amazingly relevant answer to

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:51.310
a question or application of some concept that we're learning, so, you know,

00:50:51.310 --> 00:50:55.855
your background is and your experience is one of the biggest assets that you have and,

00:50:55.855 --> 00:50:57.660
you know, nothing makes us more gratified

00:50:57.660 --> 00:50:59.730
than to see that kind of shine in a class setting.

00:50:59.730 --> 00:51:02.510
Fantastic. Zahra, is there- what would be

00:51:02.510 --> 00:51:06.235
the one thing that you would recommend everyone do in these first two weeks?

00:51:06.235 --> 00:51:09.945
Probably for many courses,

00:51:09.945 --> 00:51:11.460
for many CS courses,

00:51:11.460 --> 00:51:14.340
you need to pick the topic of your project,

00:51:14.340 --> 00:51:15.905
in the early weeks,

00:51:15.905 --> 00:51:17.750
like first two weeks or three weeks.

00:51:17.750 --> 00:51:19.060
Make sure that

00:51:19.060 --> 00:51:23.440
you pick an appropriate topic for your project.

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:25.180
Don't be too ambitious.

00:51:25.180 --> 00:51:28.755
Make like a decent goal and,

00:51:28.755 --> 00:51:30.090
make sure that,

00:51:30.090 --> 00:51:33.165
you know your teaching staff very well,

00:51:33.165 --> 00:51:34.495
make sure that

00:51:34.495 --> 00:51:36.235
you know their resources.

00:51:36.235 --> 00:51:38.655
Make sure that how you can see your grade,

00:51:38.655 --> 00:51:41.330
how you can ask the question,

00:51:41.330 --> 00:51:44.095
how you can submit your homeworks

00:51:44.095 --> 00:51:48.935
and just be on top of everything in the first two weeks of the quarter.

00:51:48.935 --> 00:51:51.675
If you have any questions,

00:51:51.675 --> 00:51:57.085
just send us an email or post on the Piazza and we'll be more than happy to answer you.

00:51:57.085 --> 00:51:58.920
Thank you. Colby?

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:01.390
I guess my advice would just be to read

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:04.500
the syllabus so a lot of courses use their own platform,

00:52:04.500 --> 00:52:06.220
whether it's Python, Matlab,

00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:07.455
